2|0@dbz^1440168412#cuap01h:havebananas$:If you look at Gohans childhood you can see why he doesn't train as an adult. From a ridiculously young age he was thrown into battles and had all manner of traumatising events happen to him. He was kidnapped, abused (as training) and saw his friends and family die around him. In the interims when Goku wasn't present he was put under all this pressure to save the world and be ready at any moment for everything to get ripped away again and the world come crashing down. Did it make him a stronger person? It did of course but it also made him a scarred and fearful young man. When he had to jump in to save the world from Cell even his immense strength couldn't keep him from making a life changing mistake and causing the death of his father - most likely his worst fear. I think considering all this he did turn out to be a well balanced person as an adult but its not surprising that he dislikes violence and anything that reminds him of those past experiences. Compared to Gokus childhood of adventure and battles he's been knocked down more than strengthened as Goku was. Hence they have different attitudes to battles. As much as his mother is seen as a nagging shrill and people dislike that he prefers the books to fighting, by choosing to study its offered him a different skill and a world that's logical and safe compared to the unpredictable chaotic world that Goku and Vegeta prefer. He is also half human and emotional without the built in drive to fight that Saiyans possess. I think he's well balanced as the show wouldn't show otherwise but all things considered its not surprising that he tries to downplay his abilities to keep away from battle. On the other hand he is Gokus son so has great loyalty amongst his good features so he'll always be dependable to do what's needed but if he can avoid it he will.
